course: dictionary
lesson: 8498
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Many words have more than one meaning, and the sentence tells you which one the writer means. The word sap is a good example. In a science lesson it names a liquid inside plants. «Look at a rose stem covered in tiny green aphids sucking sweet sap.» Aphids drink from the stem, so sap must be the plant's liquid.
explain: Sap can also be a doing word, a verb. To sap someone's energy means to slowly make them weaker or more tired. Ask yourself: is the word naming a thing, or describing an action? Then choose the meaning that fits.
explain: Sap can be informal too. «Not bad, ya sap» is Irish slang, and here Cian is being fond and teasing, calling his friend a softie or a fool. The tone of the sentence, and the friendly way it is said, help you read it correctly.
fact: Sap is the liquid inside plants and trees that carries water and food around them.
fact: Maple syrup is made from maple tree sap.
fact: «Its sap makes skin burn badly in sunlight, so never touch it.» Some plant sap irritates skin, so be careful with plants you do not know.
fact: To sap someone's energy means to slowly make them weaker or more tired.
fact: As a noun, sap can be an informal, old-fashioned word for a silly person who is easily tricked.
exercise: In «Look at a rose stem covered in tiny green aphids sucking sweet sap», what is sap? | A kind of small stone found in rivers | A piece of old wood from a fallen tree | A sort of insect that eats leaves | *A liquid inside plants that carries water and food
exercise: Maple syrup is made from which liquid? | Water from a stream | Orange juice | Milk from a cow | *Maple tree sap
exercise: What does the phrase «sap someone's energy» mean? | *To slowly make someone weaker or more tired | To give someone a big boost of strength | To make someone laugh loudly | To tell someone a secret
test: Why should you be careful around giant hogweed? | It smells very strongly of flowers | It is cold to the touch | It is too sweet to eat | *Its sap can make skin burn and blister in sunlight
test: In «Not bad, ya sap», how is sap being used? | As an angry shout | As the name of a plant | *As a fond, informal word for a softie or a fool | As a type of sweet
exam: Which meaning fits best in «The heavy homework began to sap her energy»? | To grow tall and green | To give a loud cheer | To paint the walls bright blue | *To slowly make her weaker
